<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  PROBE D6. Section 2.3.2's MANUFACTURED CYCLE: two decisionServices that require
  each other, while their MEMBER decisions have no informationRequirement between
  them. This is the hazard that constrains how coarsely sections may be grouped
  into services, and it is the runtime companion of the p6 L4-level cycle
  fixture.

      decisionService alpha = { output: a_out, input: n }
      decisionService beta  = { output: b_out, input: n }
      a_out = beta(n: n) + 1      KR to beta
      b_out = alpha(n: n) + 1     KR to alpha
      top   = alpha(n: n)         KR to alpha

  No decision requires another decision. The cycle exists ONLY between the two
  services, i.e. only on the graph section 6.3.10 would run an SCC over, and it is
  invisible to any check that looks at informationRequirement alone.

  MEASURED 2026-07-31 on this exact file, and the shape of the answer is the
  point:
    Xerces         : VALID
    KIE validator  : CLEAN. Not one message. Build clean too.
    KIE runtime    : all three decisions report status SUCCEEDED with value
                     null, AND evaluateAll additionally emits DMNResult-level
                     ERROR messages:
                       Error evaluating Decision node 'a_out': null
                       Errors occured while evaluating Decision Service node
                       'alpha'.
                     (10 errors in total across evaluateAll and the two
                     evaluateDecisionService calls.)

  SHARPENING, worth carrying back into the spec. The spec's earlier note on this
  hazard said the manufactured service cycle was caught by the harness's
  null-value rule and NOT by any error leg. On this arrangement that is too
  strong: the STATUS channel is indeed useless (SUCCEEDED on a null), and the
  VALIDATOR is indeed silent, but the RUNTIME MESSAGE channel does fire. Two of
  the three "silent" claims survive; the third does not. The practical
  consequence is unchanged, because a design-time exporter cannot see runtime
  messages, so a build-time SCC over the service graph is still the only thing
  that can catch this before deployment.

  Wired HarnessMustFail. Read the failure as "the value leg and the message leg
  both caught it, the validator did not", not as "the engine rejected the file".

  The whole file is Camunda-unparseable for D2's reason (a knowledgeRequirement
  pointing at a decisionService), so the Camunda leg measures the throw, not the
  cycle.
-->
<definitions xmlns="https://www.omg.org/spec/DMN/20191111/MODEL/" id="definitions_svc_cycle" name="svc_cycle" namespace="https://legalese.com/l4/dmn/svc_cycle" exporter="hand">
  <inputData id="input_n" name="n">
    <variable id="input_n_var" name="n" typeRef="number"/>
  </inputData>
  <decision id="decision_a_out" name="a_out">
    <variable id="decision_a_out_var" name="a_out" typeRef="number"/>
    <informationRequirement id="ir_a_out_n">
      <requiredInput href="#input_n"/>
    </informationRequirement>
    <knowledgeRequirement id="kr_a_out_beta">
      <requiredKnowledge href="#svc_beta"/>
    </knowledgeRequirement>
    <literalExpression id="decision_a_out_lit">
      <text>beta(n: n) + 1</text>
    </literalExpression>
  </decision>
  <decision id="decision_b_out" name="b_out">
    <variable id="decision_b_out_var" name="b_out" typeRef="number"/>
    <informationRequirement id="ir_b_out_n">
      <requiredInput href="#input_n"/>
    </informationRequirement>
    <knowledgeRequirement id="kr_b_out_alpha">
      <requiredKnowledge href="#svc_alpha"/>
    </knowledgeRequirement>
    <literalExpression id="decision_b_out_lit">
      <text>alpha(n: n) + 1</text>
    </literalExpression>
  </decision>
  <decision id="decision_top" name="top">
    <variable id="decision_top_var" name="top" typeRef="number"/>
    <informationRequirement id="ir_top_n">
      <requiredInput href="#input_n"/>
    </informationRequirement>
    <knowledgeRequirement id="kr_top_alpha">
      <requiredKnowledge href="#svc_alpha"/>
    </knowledgeRequirement>
    <literalExpression id="decision_top_lit">
      <text>alpha(n: n)</text>
    </literalExpression>
  </decision>
  <decisionService id="svc_alpha" name="alpha">
    <variable id="svc_alpha_var" name="alpha" typeRef="number"/>
    <outputDecision href="#decision_a_out"/>
    <inputData href="#input_n"/>
  </decisionService>
  <decisionService id="svc_beta" name="beta">
    <variable id="svc_beta_var" name="beta" typeRef="number"/>
    <outputDecision href="#decision_b_out"/>
    <inputData href="#input_n"/>
  </decisionService>
</definitions>
